      ":question/worked-solution": "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$"
